"For work assigned to an external agent, model, or separate harness session, load the installed agent-integration skill before choosing tools and use Delegate. This includes research, implementation, testing, review, consultation, comparison, and debate, including external collaboration you choose. Discover destinations through Delegate. Preserve requested model IDs, effort, and permissions; do not guess ambiguous model identifiers. For task commands, pass --root \"${DELEGATE_ROOT:-$HOME/delegation-state}\" directly in each shell invocation; reuse that root for dispatch, observation, and continuation, never an invented /tmp root. If the user explicitly requests another root, use that exact path consistently. Keep each concurrent request and its response associated separately. Capture each dispatch response and exit status once; after task_record created, observe that task ID. Never dispatch again merely to recover output or inspect its exit status. Naming a CLI identifies the destination, not a request to bypass Delegate. Earlier assistant-generated direct-launch workflows do not override this preference. Native same-harness subagents remain available unless a separate session is requested. Respect an explicit user choice of another integration; otherwise do not substitute provider CLIs, plugins, or APIs after a Delegate blocker. Before the generic task_record:not_created stop rule, handle only supervisor_upgrade_required with run_supervisor_migrate: migrate the same root once, then retry the exact original admission argv once after migrated/retry_dispatch; dispatch remains dispatch and continue remains continue, with no fresh dispatch or direct provider call replacing a failed continue. A stop_and_report failure ends that attempt: report it without retrying, diagnosing setup, or offering a fallback. Continue other independent requests. Keep collection in the foreground until completion or a reported blocker unless the user requested detachment. Pending work is observed through Delegate status, collect, or events, not OS process searches or supervisor commands. Merely discussing or configuring a tool is not delegation."
